== Summary

The \`analysis-runs-at-maximum-strictness\` standard permits an exemption in the generator's own PHPStan config only where the tool is provably blind — a property read solely through reflection, a pattern a third-party rule set cannot see through — and requires each to carry an inline comment naming the blind spot. An exemption for a real finding is forbidden.

\`phpstan.neon.dist\` currently carries seven \`ignoreErrors\` entries. Most look like genuine blind spots: reflection-only reads on runtime attribute classes and on marshalling round-trip fixtures, and a dynamic-call pattern in stubs generated by a tool paogen does not control. Some carry a comment explaining why; some do not.

Nobody has checked them against the rule since the rule was written, and an unexamined exemption list is how a strictness claim quietly becomes decoration. Two of the entries point at \`tests/generated/\*\`, which is speconaut-owned output whose generator has itself moved on — those may no longer apply at all.

== Acceptance criteria

- Every \`ignoreErrors\` entry in \`phpstan.neon.dist\` is either justified by an inline comment naming the specific tool blind spot, or removed and the underlying finding fixed.
- Each entry is verified to still be load-bearing: removing it produces a real finding. An entry that suppresses nothing is deleted.
- The two entries scoped to \`tests/generated/\*\` are re-checked against what speconaut's PHPUnit adapter emits today, since that output changed.
- The \`structural:\` reason on the standard is updated to match whatever count survives.
- \`just check\` passes.

== Non-goals

- Relaxing the emitted-tree tier, which permits no exemptions at all and is not in question here.
- Changing the PHPStan level, rule sets, or \`treatPhpDocTypesAsCertain\`.
- Refactoring the runtime attribute classes to avoid reflection. If reflection is the right design, the exemption is the right answer.

== Files

- \`phpstan.neon.dist\`
- \`truth/standards/analysis-runs-at-maximum-strictness.standard-260729.typ\`
- \`src/Runtime/Attribute/\`

== Result

The audit changed no file, which is the finding. `revert-the-strictness-relaxation`
had already paid down six of the eight real ignores earlier the same day, taking
the file from seven entries to five and rewriting the standard's `structural:`
slot to match; `runtime-package-split` then moved the attribute paths
mechanically. What was left unproven was whether the five that survived are still
load-bearing, and that is what this task establishes rather than assumes.

Method: delete one entry, run `just lint-phpstan`, record what appears, restore.
Five entries, five runs, no entry judged from its comment.

